at time  in that block and go create what would  your advice be for graduate designers  first and foremost you need real world  experience from what i've seen in the  past when i've hired  graduates recently out of school is they  understand the software and some  principles of design and animation but  they don't like they lack the  the necessary  real world experience so you need to get  that that means you can get an  internship  you can get a paid internship or get a  junior level position  at any company who's willing to give you  a shot the biggest thing is just be  transparent with where you're coming  from  let them know you just recently  graduated you don't have any real world  experience but you're eager to get it do  you often mix practical and digital  methods while working with motion i  don't i stay digital almost 100 of the  time it's pretty rare that i  scan something in  to create an asset or element for a  piece  i find that i can  get pretty much everything i need online  in some type of digital form how do i  get the willpower energy um hustle on to  do a personal project and do it good  again it kind of goes back to the  question i just had previously  which is i  love  creating motion design right it really  like fills me up it jazzes me so i enjoy  that now creating personal projects it's  more about setting aside that time to  create that personal project because we  get a lot of times get wrapped up in  doing client projects so set aside a day  on the weekend or two weeks from now and  say hey i'm going to work on this  personal project  give yourself time there's no reason  that you have to rush through a personal  project  it can take weeks or months  or even years to create a personal  project and that's okay just give it  your give it your all give it the  passion you have behind it and do  something that you really  want to do best way to get inspiration  i love getting inspiration from sites  like behance  pinterest  instagram is great as well  i also like to go look at architecture i  love to travel to different cities  different countries  that's the best way for me just to  really get inspired being around other  cultures is awesome too i look at  magazines i look at books i look at  movies a lot of different places don't  just focus on other motion design  or what other motion designers are doing  really broaden your horizon and look at  all forms of creativity out there how to  deal with imposter syndrome and share  our work on social medias yeah i get  asked this question a lot you just have  to do it you just have to post your work  on a social media channel you don't need  to post them on all the channels maybe  you pick instagram right and post one  piece you're going to be surprised at  the positive feedback you get from  people if you get a hater in there  that's really distant on you all you  have to do is block that person and just  let it go right that's going to happen  every once while but most of people are  going to support you and it's great to  get that that feedback from people  because that's how you get better faster  listening to what other people say about  your work and they give you feedback and  you take that feedback in and you go  back and you rework something or you use  it on your next project listen nobody's  perfect no one is the best we all think  we're great and then we see something  else and we're like well am i really  that good but you know what it's just  about having confidence within yourself  and just doing it just put it out there  show it to the world show the world what  you've got and be proud of it how can i  learn motion graphics at less cost i  mean i'm always going to recommend  courses i'm always going to recommend  motion science courses because we are  the best  but if you can't afford a course  youtube is going to be your best option  the problem with youtube is that you can  learn very specific things  but you don't have a road map for what  you should be learning when it's going  to take you a lot longer  to figure those things out whereas a  course is going to give you a roadmap  and say hey we need to start here we  need to end here and when you do that  you further your progress a lot quicker  but stick with youtube and you should be  okay what's your favorite kind of brief  to work on  my favorite kind of brief to work on  is one that has a lot of creative  strengths i know there's a lot of people  out there who say i want an open-ended  creative brief but ones that are very  limited like you have one image to work  with or three images to work with or one  line of type or  this color in a line of type whatever  that is  that's what makes me get the most  creative and i have to troubleshoot and  say okay how can i make this look cool  and i find that i really push myself in  those instances and a lot of times end  up making something that i'm really  proud of and is very creative why do you  not make youtube tutorials well i do  make youtube tutorials it's youtube.com  motion science and if you're here on  youtube you should be able to see them  how do i get a job in the motion area so  the best way to get a job in motion  design is to  practice practice practice get your  skill set up and then make a demo reel  once you make that demo reel you're  gonna send that reel out to  a potential client  studio agency whoever it is that you  want to work for someone that you are a  match for someone who really inspires  you and that you want to create work for  you send them your demo reel send him a  quick email and say here's who i am  here's a link to my reel  and i would love to work for you you  keep it very short and to the point but  it's all about your demo reel  that's what's going to get you hired how  are you a hello from france  hi france i absolutely love your country  i hope to be back soon is there a way to  3d project an image on a curved object  not just a plane you know as far as i  know  no there's not  in after effects you can take a flat  plane and use a cinema 4d  render engine and you can curve that  plane but you can't project once that's  a curve so your best bet is to move into  cinema 4d light  and create a curved plane in there and  project in fact we have a course that  covers cinema 4d and cinema 4d lite with  camera projection here at motion science  how do you work past a technical hang up  where in a project an unforeseen  circumstance arises that requires hours  of trial and error to solve when you're  on a tight timeline yeah if i'm working  on a project and i know that there's  some technical aspect of it that's going  to be coming up i'm going to be working  ahead of time figuring out how i'm going  to do that now some projects you're  going to work on are going to give you  time for rnd that's the time that you  spend research and developing whatever  it is you're going to be challenged with  if there's not already time  you're gonna still know the scope of the  project right whether it's a few days  whether it's a few weeks  you know what the scope of that project  is so if there's something that's  standing out to you that's gonna look  challenging  i would start on in that on trying to  tackle that as soon as possible you  should never get into a situation where  you're you're given a day and  something's kind of sprung upon you like  hey we need you to create this and you  have no idea how to create it  that's not a good client that's not a  good relationship with you and the  client that's not good communication so  you should always know what's coming up  and you should be able to kind of  balance your time and figure out how  you're going to solve that problem how  to create animation from video three  finishing touches yeah that's a really  cool animation that's actually from the  style craft course so if you haven't  checked out stylecraft yet you  definitely need to check it out so i'll  tell you what that's all the 